# Routing Enhancements for Go 1.22

Jonathan Amsterdam, on behalf of the Go team
13 February 2024

Go 1.22 brings two enhancements to `net/http` package’s router: method matching and wildcards. express common routes as patterns instead of Go code. and request.

made changes Go great language for building production systems. studied many third-party web frameworks, extracted most used features, and integrated them into `net/http`. validated choices and improved design by collaborating with community in [GitHub discussion](https://github.com/golang/go/discussions/60227) and [proposal issue](/issue/61410). Adding features to standard library means fewer dependency for many projects. third-party web frameworks remain fine choice for current users or programs advanced routing needs.

## Enhancements

routing affect pattern string passed to two `net/http.ServeMux` methods `Handle` and `HandleFunc`, and top-level functions `http.Handle` and `http.HandleFunc`. only API `net/http.Request` wildcard matches.

post integer identifier. request `GET /posts/234` retrieves post with ID 234. Before Go 1.22,

```
http.HandleFunc("/posts/", handlePost)
```

trailing slash routes all requests beginning `/posts/` to `handlePost` function, check HTTP method GET, extract identifier, and retrieve post. omit it. request `DELETE /posts/234` fetch post,

In Go 1.22, the existing code will continue to work, or you could instead write this:

```
http.HandleFunc("GET /posts/{id}", handlePost2)
```

This pattern matches GET request whose path begins “/posts/” and has two segments. (As GET matches HEAD; exactly.) `handlePost2` function no check method, and identifier string written using new `PathValue` method on `Request`:

```
idString := req.PathValue("id")
```

The rest of `handlePost2` would behave like `handlePost`, converting the string identifier to an integer and fetching the post.

Requests `DELETE /posts/234` will fail if no other matching pattern registered. [HTTP semantics](https://httpwg.org/specs/rfc9110.html#status.405), `net/http` server reply request `405 Method Not Allowed` error lists available methods `Allow` header.

A wildcard can match an entire segment, like `{id}` in the example above, or if it ends in `...` it can match all the remaining segments of the path, as in the pattern `/files/{pathname...}`.

patterns ending in slash, `/posts/`, match all paths beginning string. match only path trailing slash, write `/posts/{$}`. match `/posts/` but not `/posts` or `/posts/234`.

And there is one last bit of API: `net/http.Request` has a `SetPathValue` method so that routers outside the standard library can make the results of their own path parsing available via `Request.PathValue`.

## Precedence

Every HTTP router must deal with overlapping patterns, like `/posts/{id}` and `/posts/latest`. Both of these patterns match the path “posts/latest”, but at most one can serve the request. Which pattern takes precedence?

Some routers disallow overlaps; others use pattern registered last. Go always allowed overlaps, and chosen longer pattern regardless of registration order. order-independence important (and needed better rule than “longest wins.” rule select `/posts/latest` over `/posts/{id}`, but choose `/posts/{identifier}` over both. wildcard name shouldn’t matter. `/posts/latest` should always win matches single path instead many.

precedence rule properties patterns. preferring pattern with longest literal (non-wildcard) prefix. choose `/posts/latest` over `/posts/ {id}`. wouldn’t distinguish between `/users/{u}/posts/latest` and `/users/{u}/posts/{id}`, and should precedence.

look. Every valid pattern matches set of requests. example, `/posts/latest` matches requests with path `/posts/latest`, while `/posts/{id}` matches requests with any two-segment path whose first segment is “posts”. say one pattern is more specific than another if matches strict subset of requests. pattern `/posts/latest` is more specific than `/posts/{id}` because matches every request does, and more.

precedence rule simple: specific pattern wins. `posts/latests` should preferred to `posts/{id}`, and `/users/{u}/posts/latest` should preferred to `/users/{u}/posts/{id}`. methods. `GET /posts/{id}` takes precedence over `/posts/{id}` because first only matches GET and HEAD requests, second matches requests method.

“most specific wins” rule generalizes original “longest wins” rule for path parts of original patterns, without wildcards or `{$}`. Such patterns only overlap when one prefix of other, and longer more specific.

What if two patterns overlap but neither more specific? example, `/posts/{id}` and `/{resource}/latest` both match `/posts/latest`. no obvious answer precedence, consider conflict other. panic.

precedence rule for methods and paths, exception for hosts compatibility: if two patterns otherwise conflict and one has a host while other does not, pattern with host takes precedence.

Students computer science recall theory of regular expressions and regular languages. Each regular expression picks out a regular language, set of strings matched by the expression. Some questions easier to pose and answer by talking about languages rather than expressions. Our precedence rule inspired by theory. each routing pattern corresponds to regular expression, and sets of matching requests play role of regular languages.

Defining precedence by languages instead of expressions makes easy to state and understand. downside to infinite sets: isn’t clear how implement efficiently. determine whether two patterns conflict by walking them segment by segment. Roughly speaking, if one pattern has a literal segment wherever the other has wildcard, more specific; but if literals align with wildcards in both directions, patterns conflict.

As new patterns registered on `ServeMux`, checks for conflicts with previously registered patterns. checking every pair of patterns quadratic time. We use index to skip patterns that cannot conflict with new pattern; well. check happens when patterns registered, usually at server startup. time match incoming requests in Go 1.22 hasn’t changed from previous versions.

## Compatibility

older versions Go. new pattern syntax superset of old, and new precedence rule generalizes old one. cases. previous versions Go accepted patterns with braces and treated literally, Go 1.22 uses braces for wildcards. GODEBUG setting `httpmuxgo121` restores old behavior.

For more details about these routing enhancements, see the [`net/http.ServeMux` documentation](/pkg/net/http#ServeMux).

